   "voice": "en-US-AvaNeural",
   "text": "Can Kimchi Save Us From Microplastics.\nKimchi, the fermented vegetable dish at the heart of Korean cooking, has long been praised for its health benefits.\nIt is rich in probiotics — the helpful bacteria that support digestion.\nRecently, however, it has been connected to a much more modern problem: microplastics.\nMicroplastics are tiny fragments of plastic, often smaller than a grain of rice,\nthat form when larger plastic waste breaks down.\nThey have now been detected almost everywhere, including in drinking water, seafood, and even human blood.\nScientists are increasingly concerned about what they might do to our health over many years.\nA study from the World Institute of Kimchi attracted a lot of attention.\nResearchers isolated a single bacterium from kimchi and found that it could bind to plastic particles in the intestine.\nIn experiments with mice,\nthe animals that received this bacterium passed roughly twice as much plastic out of their bodies as those that did not.\nThe headlines were enthusiastic, suggesting that a popular side dish might quietly protect us.\nBut many experts urge caution.\nThe research was carried out on mice, used only one type of plastic,\nand is still far from proven in humans.\nMore importantly, no diet can fix a problem this large.\nThe real solution is to produce far less plastic in the first place."
