   "voice": "en-US-AvaNeural",
   "text": "Why Kimchi Won't Save Us From Microplastics.\nKimchi occupies a near-mythical place in Korean food culture, celebrated not only for its sharp,\nfermented flavour but also for a long list of supposed health benefits.\nSo when researchers suggested that a microbe found in kimchi might help rid the body of microplastics,\nthe story spread quickly — and, predictably, the coverage was glowing.\nThe science behind the buzz is genuinely intriguing.\nInvestigators at the World Institute of Kimchi isolated a lactic acid bacterium and demonstrated that,\nunder laboratory conditions designed to mimic the human gut, it clung to nanoplastic particles rather than releasing them.\nIn trials with mice, animals given the bacterium excreted roughly twice as much plastic as those that were not.\nThe implication was tantalising: that a familiar fermented food might double as a quiet detoxifier.\nYet enthusiasm should be tempered by what the study actually showed.\nThe work involved mice rather than people, tested a single type of plastic,\nand took place in simulated rather than real digestive conditions.\nTranslating such findings into a meaningful human benefit is a long and uncertain road,\nand many promising laboratory results never survive the journey.\nThere is also a deeper problem with the framing.\nMicroplastics are not a personal hygiene issue that individuals can simply eat their way out of;\nthey are a systemic consequence of how much plastic the world produces and discards.\nPinning our hopes on a side dish risks shifting responsibility away from the manufacturers and policies that drive plastic pollution.\nKimchi may be delicious and even modestly helpful,\nbut treating it as a cure lets the real culprits off the hook.\nThe only genuine remedy is to make, use, and waste far less plastic."
  },
