   "text": "Kimchi Can't Save Us From Microplastics.\nFew foods carry as much cultural weight as kimchi.\nIn Korea it is less a side dish than an institution,\nand abroad it has been steadily rebranded as a fashionable \"superfood,\" credited with everything from sharper immunity to a calmer gut.\nSo it was almost inevitable that, when scientists hinted kimchi might help flush microplastics from the body,\nthe claim would travel faster than the caveats attached to it.\nThe underlying research is far from frivolous.\nA team at the World Institute of Kimchi isolated a strain of lactic acid bacterium and showed that,\nin conditions engineered to approximate the intestine, it bound tenaciously to nanoplastic particles where rival microbes let go.\nMice given the strain excreted markedly more plastic — roughly double — than those left untreated.\nAs a piece of basic science, it is a perfectly respectable contribution to a young and urgent field.\nThe trouble lies not in the laboratory but in the leap from petri dish to dinner plate.\nThe experiments relied on mice, a single polymer, and a simulated gut; the messy reality of human digestion —\nwith its mixed meals, bile, enzymes, and resident microbial chaos — is another matter entirely.\nPreliminary findings of this kind mark the beginning of a question, not the end of one,\nand the graveyard of nutrition science is littered with compounds that dazzled in mice and disappointed in people.\nMore fundamentally, the story flatters a comforting but misleading idea:\nthat a problem manufactured at industrial scale can be undone by individual consumption.\nMicroplastics are not a personal failing to be corrected with the right breakfast;\nthey are the diffuse residue of a global economy built on disposable plastic.\nCasting a fermented vegetable as the remedy quietly relocates responsibility from the corporations and regulators who shape plastic production onto the shoulders of ordinary eaters —\na sleight of hand that suits polluters rather well.\nNone of this means kimchi is worthless; it remains nourishing, probiotic-rich and, by most accounts, delicious.\nBut there is a meaningful difference between a food that is good for you and a cure for a planetary contamination crisis.\nWe cannot eat our way out of microplastics.\nThe only credible answer is to produce far less plastic and to dispose of what we do make with far greater care —\nan unglamorous conclusion that no superfood headline will ever match."
